      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2017 American Society for Microbiology.",
        "AbstractText": "Pseudomonas aeruginosa biofilms contribute to its survival on biotic and abiotic surfaces and represent a major clinical threat due to their high tolerance to antibiotics. Therefore, the discovery of antibiofilm agents may hold great promise. We show that pharmacological inhibition of the P. aeruginosa quorum-sensing regulator MvfR (PqsR) using a benzamide-benzimidazole compound interferes with biofilm formation and potentiates biofilm sensitivity to antibiotics. Such a strategy could have great potential against P. aeruginosa persistence in diverse environments."
      },
